    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "makes sense. so if i'm looking at the beam sizing, how do i actually calculate those numbers? like, do i just add them together or is there a specific way to account for the snow load since i'm in vancouver? i dont want to end up with a beam that's too weak and sags or something.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "When sizing beams, it's not as simple as adding dead and live loads together since you have to apply factors. In Vancouver's climate, snow load must be significantly accounted for – it's actually the worst-case scenario, you have to be prepared for it. Building codes in the region, like the National Building Code of Canada (NBCC), provide specific equations and tables to determine the appropriate snow loads based on your deck’s location and other factors. You use this to scale the amount of snow load accordingly. For dead load, you'd calculate the weight of each component and translate that into a dead load in kilonewtons (kN) per meter of the deck's span. Live load will have a standard value, often referred to as 0.6 kN/m2, representing a typical occupancy weight. You factor these into the equations given by the building code to find the appropriate beam size that can handle these loads safely and without undue deflection. Consult a structural engineer or use the online resources provided by the NBCC or your local building department for exact methods and values.",
      "source": "target",
