    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Typical signs of structural failure include significant deflection (where the beam bends noticeably under weight), cracks in the decking or backing into the timber structure of the beam, loosening or settling of fixed supports, movement or pushing out of masonry or foundation elements supporting the beam, or general swaying or instability. To minimize these risks, in your initial design, ensure that the beam you choose is appropriately sized to handle the calculated loads with an adequate safety margin. You can refer to the specific section of the NBCC or contact a structural engineer, who can use more sophisticated methods to assess stress points with more accuracy. They can also recommend appropriate checking intervals and maintenance procedures to prevent any surprise failures over the deck's lifespan. Always consider professional guidance to mitigate risks effectively.",
